<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nagabhata II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 805–833</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nagabhata II was a king of the Gurjara-Pratihara dynasty of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the early 9th century. Around 816 he defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chakrayudha">Chakrayudha</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayudha-dynasty">Ayudha dynasty</a>, a client of the Pala Empire, and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kannauj</a>, which became the Pratihara capital. His reign renewed the long struggle between the Pratiharas, the Palas and the Rashtrakutas for control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges plain</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son <b>Nagabhata II</b> took Kannauj from the Ayudha dynasty around 816 and made it the imperial capital.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gurjara-pratihara">Gurjara-Pratihara · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…then by the Rashtrakuta Dhruva around 786, yet managed to place Chakrayudha on the throne of Kannauj after both rivals withdrew; around 800 the Rashtrakuta Govinda III marched north and received the submission of both Dharmapala and Chakrayudha, and <b>Nagabhata II</b> defeated Dharmapala near Monghyr and took Kannauj.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pala-empire">Pala Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>His son Govinda III repeated the feat c. 800, defeating <b>Nagabhata II</b>, while Dharmapala and his client Chakrayudha of Kannauj submitted; Rashtrakuta inscriptions claim that his horses drank from the Himalayan streams.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashtrakuta-dynasty">Rashtrakuta dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 816 <b>Nagabhata II</b> of the Gurjara-Pratihara defeated Chakrayudha and took Kannauj, which became the Pratihara capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/ayudha-dynasty">Ayudha dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kannauj</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=27.03,79.92&amp;year=819" aria-label="Kannauj on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6653 1172 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Nagabhata II"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7103 1442h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7103 1442h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=27.03,79.92&amp;year=819">What was here in 819 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
